Why Extension Methods:
•	This is the new feature that has been added in C# 3.0
•	Extension methods were introduced in C# to add new functionality to existing types without modifying their source code or creating a new derived class.
•	Problem Before Extension Methods
1.	Modify the original class
You directly change the existing class to add a new method.
public class Calculator
{
    public int Add(int a, int b)
    {
        return a + b;
    }
    // Adding new method
    public bool IsEven(int num)
    {
        return num % 2 == 0;
    }
}
Why this is a problem?
•	You may not own the class (like string, int) 
•	Modifying existing code can: 
•	Break other functionality 
•	Violate Open/Closed Principle 
•	Not possible for built-in .NET classes
1. “You may not own the class (like string, int)” — Meaning
public class MyClass
{
    public void Test() { }
}
You own this class → you can edit it anytime
But for built-in types like string, int, etc, these come from .NET Framework (library), you did NOT create them, so you don’t own them
So you cannot do this:
public class string
{
     public bool IsLong()
     {
          return this.Length > 5;
     }
}
Because:
•	string is already defined inside .NET 
•	You can’t open and edit its source code

2.	“Not possible for built-in .NET classes” — Meaning
Built-in classes like:
•	string 
•	int 
•	DateTime 
•	List<T> 
are:
•	Already compiled inside .NET 
•	Often marked as sealed
So, this is NOT allowed:
class MyString : string // Error
{
}
How Extension Methods Solve This
•	We will write a extension class inside that class we can write properties and methods 
•	But the method should be static
•	“this” keyword is important, this only bind/ attach our logic into the existing(built-in method or properties) 
•	Extension methods allow us to add new methods to existing types like string without modifying or inheriting them, and they are called like instance methods but executed as static methods internally.
